<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle Ages</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500–<a href="/years/1500">1500</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as medieval period</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Middle Ages is the period of European history between the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> in the 5th century and the beginning of the early modern era around 1500. It is usually divided into early, high and late phases. The term is also applied loosely to the same centuries elsewhere, as when histories describe the rise of local dynasties or the abandonment of ancient towns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/middle-ages.jpg" alt="Middle Ages" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules &amp;amp; Jenny from Lincoln, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury_Cathedral,_window_nXV_detail_(46220634195).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
